=== phase 06: multi-port 16550 serial + RS485 declaration ===
date:        2026-10-05
iso:         /home/dmin/cellos/build/vicell-x86.iso
iso sha256:  ad9f56d90d265f3f21fa2ad791d7bd11f61ad8005154cbebe792bb7788b74397
qemu:        QEMU emulator version 8.2.2 (Debian 1:8.2.2+ds-0ubuntu1.17)
tracing:     DISABLED (no -trace/-d arguments on any run)

The board profile declares the four standard PC COM ports; the kernel probes each
one and offers only the ports that answer, and `/bin/serial` (holding the
`serial_port` capability) drives exactly those. The console stays COM1 and the
polled-RX fallback is untouched.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN 1 — kernel probe, default q35 (only COM1 exists)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
[ INFO] [x86-gate] serial port 0 base=0x3f8 irq=4 usable
[ WARN] [x86-gate] serial port 1 base=0x2f8 irq=3 absent (16550 probe failed)
[ WARN] [x86-gate] serial port 2 base=0x3e8 irq=4 absent (16550 probe failed)
[ WARN] [x86-gate] serial port 3 base=0x2e8 irq=3 absent (16550 probe failed)
USER: Cellos >

A declared port the machine does not have is refused **by name**; nothing assumes
it exists, and the console is unaffected (the shell prompt still appears).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN 2 — kernel probe with `-device isa-serial` on indices 1..3
--------------------------------------------------------------------
[ INFO] [x86-gate] serial port 0 base=0x3f8 irq=4 usable
[ INFO] [x86-gate] serial port 1 base=0x2f8 irq=3 usable
[ INFO] [x86-gate] serial port 2 base=0x3e8 irq=4 usable
[ INFO] [x86-gate] serial port 3 base=0x2e8 irq=3 usable

The probe accepts a real 16550 register set (scratch register + divisor latch
read-back) and the same machine now offers all four ports.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN 3 — `/bin/serial` on a machine with COM1+COM2 (COM2 behind a socket chardev)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
USER: [serial] port 0 base=0x03f8 irq=4 usable
USER: [serial] port 1 base=0x02f8 irq=3 usable
USER: [serial] port 2 base=0x03e8 irq=4 absent (kernel probe refused)
USER: [serial] port 3 base=0x02e8 irq=3 absent (kernel probe refused)
[serial] port 0 marker base=0x03f8
USER: [serial] port 0 tx marker bytes=36
USER: [serial] port 1 tx marker bytes=36
USER: [serial] port 1 rx byte=0x5a echoed
USER: Cellos > [serial] serial cell ready: usable=2 declared=4 echoed=1

Host side, on the COM2 chardev socket (the test harness's view):

TX (guest -> host):            b'[serial] port 1 marker base=0x02f8\r\n'
RX + echo (host -> guest -> host): b'Z'

So the transmit path carries cell bytes to the far end of the port, and a byte
injected into the port comes back echoed by the cell — the port is usable **from a
cell** in both directions, not just for kernel console output. The console is
untouched in the same boot (`Cellos >` appears once, on COM1).

